{
  "source": "Bies 5e Table C.1, PDF pages 747-750 (printed pp. 718-721)",
  "about": "One hundred and five solids from Appendix C, \"Properties of Materials and Gases\", in the five groups the page prints in bold: metals, building materials, wood, plastics and other, and aluminium honeycomb. The table's first three rows are air, fresh water and sea water, which are not solids and are not here. Column 4 is what the page calls \"Speed of sound for a 1-D solid\", sqrt(E/rho), so it is the bar speed; the closing note prints the plate and unbounded forms too. The page says the column was calculated from the modulus and the density, and it reproduces to one per cent on seventy-nine of the eighty-seven rows that print both as single values; three rows do not follow from their own two columns and carry that in their note. The loss factor column holds two numbers, not a range: footnote a says to use the upper limit for panels in building construction and the lower for panels welded together in an enclosure, so the low end is the internal loss factor and the high end the in-situ one. Poisson ratios the sources did not give were calculated by the authors from the three-dimensional speed, and for some plastics taken from a similar material, but the page does not say which cells those are, so none is marked. The values are, in the book's own words, representative only.",
